# Report: host functions now render as a named, portable marker, locally and when served

A host function now prints as `{:type :host-fn, :name '+}` (or `'dao.space.query/q` after the require) instead of `#object[...]`. The full JVM and CLJS lanes pass; cljstyle was blocked by permissions and not run. The work is in the main tree at master 04378221, not staged or committed. The only source file changed is `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`; `yin.vm/*`, `yin/repl/query.cljc` and `dao/data.cljc` are untouched. The full report is at `collab/1790704328000-vm-engineer-host-fn-rendering.claude-opus-5-5.report.md`.

## The form

```
{:type :host-fn, :name '+}
{:type :host-fn, :name 'dao.space.query/q}
{:type :host-fn}                      ; when no name is known
```

- **Why this form:** it has the same shape as `{:type :closure, ...}`, and the `:type` key tells the two apart.
- **Same on every host:** it goes through the same pretty printer and symbol quoting as everything else, so CLJ, CLJS and CLJD print the same text.
- **Why not a tag:** a form like `#host-fn[+]` would be the REPL's only tagged output, and no host's reader could read it back.

## How names are found

Names are looked up by identity in the VM's own tables, with no per-host reflection.
- **Primitives:** the existing `yin.vm/name-of`. The de Bruijn stack and register VMs don't carry an alias table, so the lookup falls back to `yin.vm/primitive-canonical-names`. Without that, `==` rendered nameless on those two VMs.
- **Module exports:** read from the VM's module registry, named as `module/export`. That is how `dao.space.query/q` gets its name after the require, with no change to `query.cljc`.

## Served sessions

No `dao.data` change was needed. A served endpoint sends back the text the server's REPL already rendered, so a served session shows the same string as a local one. A new test checks that.

## Tests and verification

**New tests:**
- `repl_test`: on all four VMs, checks `+`, `dao.space.query/q` after require, host functions inside a collection built at runtime, `==` rendering as `=`, and that a closure still renders as `{:type :closure ...}`. A second test covers the nameless marker.
- `serve_test`: served answers must equal the local session's text.
- Every new test also asserts no output contains `#object[`.

**Mutation proofs:** I applied each break on its own, checked that the tests caught it, then reverted it.

| Break | Result |
|---|---|
| Remove the host-function branch | `#object[...]` came back and the tests failed |
| Ignore module exports | 5 failures |
| Render results without names | 18 failures |

